COMPLEMENTARY_IMAGES = """**COMPLEMENTARY IMAGE SELECTION**

Within the existing 4–8-image search, assess photographs for both the hero and a possible supporting role. Compare a strong single-image composition with one using complementary imagery. When a source naturally sequences or flows with the hero and adds a different part of the story, use it as subordinate background imagery if it improves the complete composition without clutter.

A celebration can pair with authentic playing action; an action hero can pair with a meaningful emotional portrait. Consider a defining skill, decisive moment, reaction or relationship that the hero does not already communicate. Give each photograph a distinct purpose. Keep the hero dominant through differences in scale, contrast, colour and placement; keep supporting faces, gestures and equipment recognisable at their intended size. A source-based portrait or action silhouette can support emotion and depth; an invented shadow face or a repeated oversized transparent head cannot substitute for an authentic source.

Apply the same original-source, image-quality and photographic-authenticity checks to every supporting subject as to the hero, including preservation of facial features. Choose compatible team, uniform, era, race or event context, visual style, camera angle and lighting. For an event-specific design, supporting photographs must belong to that verified event. For a career or club-identity tribute, compatible moments from the relevant era may form an evident artistic montage without implying that they happened together. Preserve genuine interactions and sequences rather than inventing a shared moment.

Rebalance the environment, text and accents around supporting imagery instead of layering it over an already busy arrangement. Keep a single image when it tells the story more powerfully or no compatible supporting source is available. Complete selection within the existing research allowance and continue with the strongest available sources.

"""

